To check the files, get the tag and compare:

  git clone --depth 1 --branch stable-v1.0.3 \
      https://github.com/cr-marcstevens/sha1collisiondetection.git sha1dc
  (cd sha1dc/lib && sha256sum sha1.c sha1.h ubc_check.c ubc_check.h)
  sha256sum c_src/cosmo/sha1dc/*.c c_src/cosmo/sha1dc/*.h

The later commits of the branch master change the detection of the byte
order of big-endian CPUs, the access to unaligned data, and the names of
macros. The detection of collisions is the same. beam_com_sha1dc.c sets
SHA1DC_FORCE_LITTLEENDIAN: cosmocc makes code only for x86_64 and
aarch64, and both are little-endian. Each part that beam_com_sha1dc.c
gives to SHA1DCUpdate() starts at an aligned address, and each part
except the last is a multiple of 64 bytes.
